    {
      "id": "s0004",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Creates or updates a Systems Manager resource policy. A resource policy helps you to define the IAM entity (for example, an Amazon Web Services account) that can manage your Systems Manager resources. The following resources support Systems Manager resource policies.",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s0005",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "[[p0]] - The resource policy for [[p1]] enables Amazon Web Services accounts to view and interact with OpsCenter operational work items (OpsItems).",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "[[p0]]",
          "inline": {
            "tag": "code",
            "text": "OpsItemGroup"
          }
        },
        {
          "token": "[[p1]]",
          "inline": {
            "tag": "code",
            "text": "OpsItemGroup"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s0006",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "[[p0]] - The resource policy is used to share a parameter with other accounts using Resource Access Manager (RAM). To share a parameter, it must be in the advanced parameter tier. For information about parameter tiers, see [[p1]] . For information about changing an existing standard parameter to an advanced parameter, see [[p2]] . To share a [[p3]] parameter, it must be encrypted with a customer managed key, and you must share the key separately through Key Management Service. Amazon Web Services managed keys cannot be shared. Parameters encrypted with the default Amazon Web Services managed key can be updated to use a customer managed key instead. For KMS key definitions, see [[p4]] in the [[p5]]Key Management Service Developer Guide[[p6]] .",
      "protected": [
        {
